# Run the button detector once, on demand, and return what it saw.
#
# The node also publishes detections continuously on /<source>/elevator_buttons.
# That stream is the right thing to watch; this service is the right thing to
# *ask*, and the difference matters for a caller like the press_elevator_button
# primitive. Inference costs seconds per frame, so a subscriber has no way to
# tell "the newest message is 4 s old because inference is slow" from "the
# newest message is 4 s old because the arm moved and nothing has been looked
# at since". A service call is unambiguous: the reply describes a frame that
# was captured after the request was made.

# Camera to look through. Empty uses the node's current active_source.
# Naming a different source retargets the node, exactly as setting the
# active_source parameter would, and the change persists after the call.
string source

# Detector confidence floor for this call only. <= 0 uses the node's
# configured score_threshold.
float32 score_threshold

# How long to wait for a frame newer than this request, plus the inference it
# then has to run. <= 0 uses the node's detect_timeout_s parameter.
# A retarget needs the new camera's first frame to arrive, so allow for that.
float32 timeout_s
---
# False when no answer could be produced: unknown source, no frame arrived
# before the timeout, or inference raised. `detections` is empty in that case.
# A frame that was looked at and simply had no buttons in it is a SUCCESS with
# an empty array -- "I looked and saw nothing" is not a failure.
bool success

# Human-readable reason, always set on failure and usually empty on success.
string message

# Header, source, image dimensions, inference cost and the boxes themselves.
# Identical in content to what went out on the topic for the same frame.
evo_interfaces/ButtonDetectionArray detections
